# Cloudflare Pages security headers. Netlify (and netlify.toml, which this
# was translated from) are fully retired — this is the only copy now.
#
# Rule blocks are kept comment-free and blank-line-separated below (no
# comments between a path pattern and its header lines, or between two
# blocks) after an earlier version of this file — sitting uncommitted
# locally, never actually deployed — turned out untestable for exactly
# this until it finally shipped. Once live, X-Frame-Options was silently
# dropped even though X-Content-Type-Options and Referrer-Policy from the
# same block came through fine, and the /staff/* block didn't apply at
# all — cause unconfirmed (Cloudflare's own docs don't document _headers
# parse-error behavior for malformed/interleaved lines), so this rewrite
# removes the interleaved comments as the most likely culprit and adds a
# CSP fallback for the framing protection in case X-Frame-Options really
# is filtered rather than just a parsing casualty.
#
# css/*, js/*: this site has no content-hashed filenames (css/styles.css
# is always css/styles.css), so with no explicit Cache-Control a browser
# falls back to heuristic caching and can keep serving a stylesheet from
# before the last deploy for a long time -- confirmed directly: a CSS fix
# looked live in a fresh incognito window but not in an already-open
# regular tab. no-cache (not no-store) still lets the browser cache the
# file, it just forces a revalidation request every time, which Cloudflare
# answers with a cheap 304 off the same ETag when the file hasn't
# actually changed -- so this trades a little freshness-check overhead
# for every deploy being visible on the next real page load instead of
# needing a hard refresh.

/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

/css/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/js/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/staff/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
